- Chapter 5: Confidence intervals -- the dual of tests
    - Carried from Chapter 4
        - Tests answer "can I rule out this single value of p?". Intervals invert the question: "what set of values can I NOT rule out?"
- Loop A: five intervals on the same data
    - What does "95%" mean? Frequentist vs Bayesian
        - A frequentist 95% confidence interval is a statement about the *procedure*, not the parameter. If we repeated the experiment many times and built a fresh CI each time, 95% of those intervals would contain the true p. The interval [0.30, 0.90] does NOT mean "p is in [0.30, 0.90] with probability 0.95". The parameter p is fixed (unknown but not random), and the interval is the random object that wraps around it 95% of the time.
        - A Bayesian 95% credible interval IS a probability statement about p. Given the prior and the data, we say p falls in [0.32, 0.81] with posterior probability 0.95. The data are fixed, the parameter is uncertain, and the interval just slices off the middle 95% of the posterior.
        - These two intervals can numerically agree to three decimal places and still mean different things. Keep that in mind as we line them up below.
    - Try
        - For 6/10, 60/100, 600/1000 compute five 95% intervals: Wald, Wilson, Clopper-Pearson, percentile bootstrap, Bayesian credible (Beta(1,1) prior).
    - Observe
        - At 6/10 the intervals are wide and a little different from each other: Wald [0.30, 0.90], Wilson [0.31, 0.83], Clopper-Pearson [0.26, 0.88], bootstrap [0.30, 0.90], Bayesian [0.32, 0.81]. They all contain 0.5 -- consistent with our earlier "no signal" conclusion.
        - At 60/100 the intervals tighten to about [0.50, 0.69]. The methods are now nearly indistinguishable. 0.5 sits just barely above the lower edge (Wilson lower bound is approximately 0.502), the borderline result we've been chasing.
        - At 600/1000 the intervals collapse to about [0.57, 0.63]. 0.5 is far away.
        - ![Method comparison](images/ci_method_comparison.png)
    - Hunch
        - At moderate-to-large N, the choice of interval method does not matter for almost any decision. At small N or at extremes, the choices matter and the bad ones get embarrassingly wrong.
    - A note on the percentile bootstrap
        - The percentile bootstrap is a benign default in symmetric, non-boundary cases like 60/100. It is biased on skewed sampling distributions and near 0 or 1, which is exactly where Wald already fails. The bias-corrected and accelerated (BCa) variant exists for those regimes; we use the simpler percentile form here and flag the caveat. We will use this idea again in Chapters 8, 13, and 16, each time on a different statistic.
- Loop B: edge cases break Wald
    - Try
        - 0/10, 10/10, 0/100, 100/100. Compute the same intervals.
    - Observe
        - Wald at 0/10: the interval is [0, 0]. Technically you cannot rule out *anything*, but Wald says you can rule out everything. This is the famous Wald failure mode.
        - Wilson at 0/10: [0.000, 0.278]. Sensible: I cannot say p > 0.28.
        - Clopper-Pearson at 0/10: [0.000, 0.308]. Conservative.
        - Bayesian at 0/10 with Beta(1, 1) prior: posterior is Beta(1, 11), 95% credible [0.002, 0.265]. Honest about uncertainty.
        - ![Boundary failures](images/boundary_failures.png)
    - Rule of thumb
        - Don't use Wald near boundaries. Don't use Wald at small N. A sharper version of "small": Wald is unreliable when N * hat-p < 5 or N * (1 - hat-p) < 5, and is degenerate (collapses to a single point) when hat-p is exactly 0 or 1.
        - Wilson is a good default. Clopper-Pearson is safe but conservative. Bayesian with a flat Beta(1, 1) prior is shape-similar to Clopper-Pearson but a bit narrower; the Jeffreys prior Beta(0.5, 0.5) matches Clopper-Pearson more tightly (Chapter 6 introduces Jeffreys priors formally; here we just need the comparison). Bootstrap (percentile) is fine when you don't have a closed-form analytic option, with the caveat above for skewed or boundary cases.
- Loop C: priors that argue
    - Try
        - 60 heads in 100 tosses, viewed through three different priors: flat Beta(1,1), skeptical Beta(50,50), tail-leaning Beta(2,8).
    - Observe
        - Flat prior posterior: Beta(61, 41), centred at 0.598, 95% CI roughly [0.50, 0.69].
        - Skeptical prior posterior: Beta(110, 90), centred at 0.55, 95% CI roughly [0.48, 0.62]. The data moved us, but only halfway -- the prior was strong.
        - Tail-leaning prior posterior: Beta(62, 48), centred at 0.564. It's harder to drag a "I expected tails" prior all the way to 0.6 with only 100 tosses.
        - ![Three priors](images/three_priors.png)
    - Hunch
        - Priors are a feature, not a bug. They make assumptions explicit. The frequentist's *implicit* prior (in this setting, "no prior, p is whatever value the test is asking about") is not less of an assumption -- it's just less visible.
- Loop D: do these intervals actually cover the truth?
    - Try
        - Simulate 5,000 fair-coin experiments at each of N in {5, 10, 20, 50, 100}. For each one, compute Wald, Wilson, Clopper-Pearson, and Bayesian 95% intervals. Count how often each contains the true p = 0.5.
    - Observe
        - At N = 5 Wald's coverage drops well below the nominal 0.95 line, while Wilson, Clopper-Pearson, and Bayesian stay near or above it.
        - At N = 10 the gap narrows but Wald is still visibly low; the others remain near 0.95.
        - At N = 20, 50, 100 all four methods cluster around 0.95 to 0.96. The differences are small here because Wald's failure modes (counts at the boundary 0 or N) become rare.
        - ![Coverage simulation across N](images/coverage_simulation.png)
        - Reproducibility: seed = 123, 5000 trials per N at p = 0.5.
    - Hunch
        - "95%" only means what you think it means if the construction is honest about the model. Wald isn't, at small N. Wilson stays near 95% across the N grid. Clopper-Pearson is conservative on purpose.
- Loop D': where exactly does Wald lie?
    - Try
        - Hold N fixed at 30. Sweep the true p across a grid from 0.02 to 0.98. At each p, simulate 3,000 binomial experiments and record empirical 95%-CI coverage for Wald, Wilson, Clopper-Pearson, and Bayesian.
    - Observe
        - Wald's coverage curve oscillates in p. It dips well below 0.95 at small p and at large p, with sharp non-monotone wiggles in between, even at this moderate N. Coverage at p = 0.5 alone hides the failure.
        - Wilson stays close to 0.95 across the whole range, with mild wiggles but no systematic dip.
        - Clopper-Pearson sits at or above 0.95 everywhere, the price of its guaranteed conservatism.
        - Bayesian (flat prior) tracks Clopper-Pearson in shape, slightly tighter.
        - ![Coverage vs true p](images/coverage_vs_p.png)
        - Reproducibility: seed = 456, 3000 trials per p, N fixed at 30.
    - Hunch
        - Single-point coverage checks (like "at p = 0.5") are the wrong test. The right question is whether coverage holds across the parameter space. Wald fails this test at any moderate N, which is why textbooks built around Wald give the wrong intuition for confidence in small-sample binomial work.
- The big question that opens Chapter 6
    - We've used "posterior" half a dozen times. Time to make it formal: a prior, a likelihood, a posterior, a credible interval, a Bayes factor. Up to now we've used the closed-form Beta-binomial conjugate result. From here on we use PyMC because most real models don't have a closed form.
    - Big question: what does the Bayesian framework actually look like, taken seriously and end-to-end?
